WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.282 --> 00:00:03.510
- On this episode, I talk
about the Apple Watch,

00:00:03.510 --> 00:00:07.132
setting goals, and I get deep.

00:00:07.132 --> 00:00:12.132
(dramatic music)

00:00:12.983 --> 00:00:15.312
Hey, everybody, this is Gary Vay-ner-chuk,

00:00:15.312 --> 00:00:20.065
and you're watching the 
#AskGaryVee Show, Episode 16.

00:00:20.065 --> 00:00:22.389
Fun fact about the number 16.

00:00:22.389 --> 00:00:24.361
I dislike the number.

00:00:24.361 --> 00:00:26.288
- [Voiceover] Santiago asks,

00:00:26.288 --> 00:00:29.144
"What do you think about the Apple Watch?"

00:00:29.144 --> 00:00:32.325
- Santiago, I, I've been
here in Atlantic City

00:00:32.325 --> 00:00:34.531
all week and did not
watch the performance,

00:00:34.531 --> 00:00:36.713
so I actually know very
little about the Apple Watch.

00:00:36.713 --> 00:00:38.385
I think it's kind of ironic
that Steve's asking me

00:00:38.385 --> 00:00:39.964
this question.

00:00:39.964 --> 00:00:43.325
But I chose to answer it, and here's why.

00:00:43.325 --> 00:00:45.049
I'm gonna answer it philosophically.

00:00:45.049 --> 00:00:46.465
I actually didn't see all
the bells and whistles.

00:00:46.465 --> 00:00:48.508
I literally know nothing
about the Apple Watch.

00:00:48.508 --> 00:00:50.598
I saw like some random
picture in a Twitter feed,

00:00:50.598 --> 00:00:52.131
but here's what I do know.

00:00:52.131 --> 00:00:55.675
This becomes this.

00:00:55.675 --> 00:00:59.031
This becomes this.

00:00:59.031 --> 00:01:01.441
And then this becomes this.

00:01:01.441 --> 00:01:04.855
We are absolutely 100% going robotic.

00:01:04.855 --> 00:01:06.480
And I mean over the course of the next

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:10.056
100 plus years and so the evolution of

00:01:10.056 --> 00:01:12.354
information being closer to us

00:01:12.354 --> 00:01:13.956
started with something
that looked like this,

00:01:13.956 --> 00:01:14.862
well, further back,

00:01:14.862 --> 00:01:16.696
you go back and then evolve to something

00:01:16.696 --> 00:01:17.764
that was always on us.

00:01:17.764 --> 00:01:20.226
Now it's gonna be like even closer,

00:01:20.226 --> 00:01:22.747
like not even going in the pocket.

00:01:22.747 --> 00:01:23.964
Google Glass and things of that nature

00:01:23.964 --> 00:01:25.102
I think are underrated.

00:01:25.102 --> 00:01:27.238
I think more of that kind
of stuff is coming as well.

00:01:27.238 --> 00:01:29.792
And so I think it's evolution.

00:01:29.792 --> 00:01:31.069
The human evolution.

00:01:31.069 --> 00:01:32.021
- Hey, Gary, how's it going.

00:01:32.021 --> 00:01:33.089
My name is Adorian Deck.

00:01:33.089 --> 00:01:34.877
I make YouTube videos, and I created the

00:01:34.877 --> 00:01:36.316
Twitter account, OMG Facts.

00:01:36.316 --> 00:01:38.267
Now I control and manage a
network of many different

00:01:38.267 --> 00:01:40.403
accounts on many different
social platforms.

00:01:40.403 --> 00:01:42.075
And my question for you today is

00:01:42.075 --> 00:01:43.723
do you believe creating my own website

00:01:43.723 --> 00:01:45.975
with valuable and original
content to drive traffic to

00:01:45.975 --> 00:01:48.994
is a reasonable investment
for long-term branding?

00:01:48.994 --> 00:01:50.248
Or do you believe that
websites are going to be

00:01:50.248 --> 00:01:52.407
irrelevant eventually with
this mobile revolution

00:01:52.407 --> 00:01:53.173
that's happening.

00:01:53.173 --> 00:01:54.915
Should I be looking more
into app development

00:01:54.915 --> 00:01:57.144
or something that I'm not even looking at?

00:01:57.144 --> 00:01:59.330
Let me know, thanks.

00:01:59.330 --> 00:02:00.139
- You know, I think that's
a really good question

00:02:00.139 --> 00:02:01.648
a lot of people are struggling with.

00:02:01.648 --> 00:02:03.018
I will say this.

00:02:03.018 --> 00:02:05.758
That, you know, I have garyvaynerchuk.com.

00:02:05.758 --> 00:02:07.824
Oh no, you can't link
it out, but link there.

00:02:07.824 --> 00:02:10.711
Anyway, I have
garyvaynerchuk.com for a reason.

00:02:10.711 --> 00:02:11.818
Not the kicks and giggles.

00:02:11.818 --> 00:02:15.208
I want to have a platform
that I fundamentally control

00:02:15.208 --> 00:02:16.462
all aspects of.

00:02:16.462 --> 00:02:19.457
And so when you are
building brand on Twitter

00:02:19.457 --> 00:02:21.733
with a bunch of Twitter accounts
like you are, like I am,

00:02:21.733 --> 00:02:23.242
when you're building a Facebook fan page

00:02:23.242 --> 00:02:25.239
and then all of a sudden
Facebook deems for itself

00:02:25.239 --> 00:02:28.095
and for its audience and
for you, believe it or not,

00:02:28.095 --> 00:02:30.742
that they need to drop down organic reach

00:02:30.742 --> 00:02:32.646
and everything you're putting
out is not being delivered,

00:02:32.646 --> 00:02:34.898
you start understanding why
having your own email service,

00:02:34.898 --> 00:02:36.477
having your own website, matters.

00:02:36.477 --> 00:02:38.102
Having your own app matters

00:02:38.102 --> 00:02:40.447
if you deem that people are
gonna spend more of their time

00:02:40.447 --> 00:02:44.928
within the app than on
a mobile native website.

00:02:44.928 --> 00:02:48.179
Just because the world's
going mobile or wristy

00:02:48.179 --> 00:02:50.826
or this, you know, or virtual reality,

00:02:50.826 --> 00:02:53.496
doesn't necessarily mean
that the website is gone.

00:02:53.496 --> 00:02:56.515
It's really just protocol
for a place that you can own.

00:02:56.515 --> 00:02:58.581
And so what I would say is
philosophically, you wanna

00:02:58.581 --> 00:03:00.694
have your own house.

00:03:00.694 --> 00:03:04.061
But doing business in your
vacation home or in a hotel

00:03:04.061 --> 00:03:05.106
also mattered.

00:03:05.106 --> 00:03:08.367
If you understood that
analogy, you're on your way.

00:03:08.367 --> 00:03:09.773
- [Voiceover] Kyle asks,
"Do you set and track

00:03:09.773 --> 00:03:12.698
"personal/business goals -
aside from owning the Jets?

00:03:12.698 --> 00:03:15.276
"What do you find useful in the process?"

00:03:15.276 --> 00:03:18.085
You know, I really don't
set any business goals

00:03:18.085 --> 00:03:21.545
other than to continue to gain leverage.

00:03:21.545 --> 00:03:23.263
You know, pay forward.

00:03:23.263 --> 00:03:25.167
Provide value to the audience.

00:03:25.167 --> 00:03:26.560
Make money.

00:03:26.560 --> 00:03:28.882
Keep it practical and ambitious.

00:03:28.882 --> 00:03:30.600
But I don't have a goal of like,

00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:33.224
"I'm gonna get VaynerMedia to 100 million

00:03:33.224 --> 00:03:34.385
"and then this is gonna happen,"

00:03:34.385 --> 00:03:37.496
or "The fund is gonna return three times

00:03:37.496 --> 00:03:40.097
"its 25 million dollar
investment at Vayner RSE

00:03:40.097 --> 00:03:41.467
"and then this will happen,"

00:03:41.467 --> 00:03:43.835
or, "In four years, I'm gonna get into the

00:03:43.835 --> 00:03:45.251
"car wash business."

00:03:45.251 --> 00:03:47.307
I just don't go that route.

00:03:47.307 --> 00:03:49.617
My route is really my fundamental play is

00:03:49.617 --> 00:03:51.706
I always talk about my
one goal being the Jets

00:03:51.706 --> 00:03:54.116
and for the hardcore people
that followed my career,

00:03:54.116 --> 00:03:55.561
I'm gonna throw a little
bit of a curveball to you,

00:03:55.561 --> 00:03:56.768
give you a little more fun fact.

00:03:56.768 --> 00:03:58.672
That's kinda the thing that
I wanna do with this show.

00:03:58.672 --> 00:04:01.435
The only other core strategy I have is

00:04:01.435 --> 00:04:03.977
collecting people.

00:04:03.977 --> 00:04:05.963
You know, finding the
individuals that I think

00:04:05.963 --> 00:04:08.137
I can jam with for the next

00:04:08.137 --> 00:04:11.171
15, 20, 30, 45

00:04:11.171 --> 00:04:12.844
years professionally.

00:04:12.844 --> 00:04:14.740
That to me is the global strategy.

00:04:14.740 --> 00:04:18.315
Who can I do business with
as a teammate forever.

00:04:19.144 --> 00:04:20.046
- What's up, Gary.

00:04:20.046 --> 00:04:22.355
So, you know, I just quit my job at radio

00:04:22.355 --> 00:04:25.560
after being on air since
I was 16 years old.

00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:28.090
I kinda risked everything
for this new Dash Radio app.

00:04:28.090 --> 00:04:28.903
I hope you've checked it out.

00:04:28.903 --> 00:04:30.157
You've gotta tell me
your favorite station.

00:04:30.157 --> 00:04:32.595
But what was the riskiest
thing you've ever

00:04:32.595 --> 00:04:33.895
done in your career?

00:04:33.895 --> 00:04:34.754
Because it was tough for me to leave

00:04:34.754 --> 00:04:35.938
something I've been at for so long,

00:04:35.938 --> 00:04:37.912
especially an institution
like traditional radio,

00:04:37.912 --> 00:04:40.025
in order to try something new with zero

00:04:40.025 --> 00:04:42.161
listeners today to disrupt that industry.

00:04:42.161 --> 00:04:44.506
What was the toughest choice you ever made

00:04:44.506 --> 00:04:46.967
in your life or in business
about leaving something

00:04:46.967 --> 00:04:50.311
that you've done to do something new?

00:04:51.541 --> 00:04:54.679
- You know, again, the

00:04:54.679 --> 00:04:57.707
enormous excitement I have

00:04:57.707 --> 00:04:59.181
behind doing this show
and thank you all for

00:04:59.181 --> 00:05:01.897
allowing me to do this show,

00:05:01.897 --> 00:05:03.848
is predicated on the fact
that I get to tell you

00:05:03.848 --> 00:05:05.009
things you've never heard before.

00:05:05.009 --> 00:05:07.888
I'm gonna say something
that I've never ever

00:05:07.888 --> 00:05:12.485
ever ever ever ever have
talked about before.

00:05:12.485 --> 00:05:15.016
Which is the toughest
professional thing I've ever done.

00:05:15.016 --> 00:05:17.454
My cliche answer historically
has been, you know,

00:05:17.454 --> 00:05:20.654
leaving the Wine Library
with is my first love,

00:05:20.654 --> 00:05:21.796
and that's a very big truth.

00:05:21.796 --> 00:05:24.280
But as I've started becoming

00:05:24.280 --> 00:05:26.440
a little bit more in
tune with my own feelings

00:05:26.440 --> 00:05:28.878
and have given this thought,

00:05:28.878 --> 00:05:30.248
the toughest thing I ever had to do,

00:05:30.248 --> 00:05:31.525
and Steve, pay attention to me.

00:05:31.525 --> 00:05:32.201
You'll like this answer.

00:05:32.201 --> 00:05:33.754
The toughest thing I ever had to do,

00:05:33.754 --> 00:05:35.193
believe it or not,

00:05:35.193 --> 00:05:37.655
was make the decision that I was okay with

00:05:37.655 --> 00:05:40.468
putting myself out there.

00:05:40.468 --> 00:05:43.227
You know, a lot of you
probably have at this point

00:05:43.227 --> 00:05:46.129
forgotten because the Gary Vee persona and

00:05:46.129 --> 00:05:48.893
narrative has taken over
my career and my Wikipedia

00:05:48.893 --> 00:05:50.564
and things of that nature.

00:05:50.564 --> 00:05:54.837
But the first 10 to 15
articles that have ever

00:05:54.837 --> 00:05:57.716
been written about me in the
AP and the New York Times

00:05:57.716 --> 00:05:59.341
and the Wall Street Journal was,

00:05:59.341 --> 00:06:01.965
"Wiz Kid Builds Business."

00:06:01.965 --> 00:06:04.473
That the establishment,
the business world,

00:06:04.473 --> 00:06:08.055
the people that I look up to in ways,

00:06:08.055 --> 00:06:09.510
maybe not specifically because I don't

00:06:09.510 --> 00:06:10.716
really have heroes, but

00:06:10.716 --> 00:06:15.502
respect their opinions
and their accomplishments.

00:06:16.102 --> 00:06:18.241
I went from the narrative of being a great

00:06:18.241 --> 00:06:21.956
business operator that
saw trends and executed it

00:06:21.956 --> 00:06:24.464
for financial gain,
which is like something

00:06:24.464 --> 00:06:27.436
everybody deems to be so noble in business

00:06:27.436 --> 00:06:30.342
to being a self promoter,

00:06:30.342 --> 00:06:34.154
to being a social media guru.

00:06:34.484 --> 00:06:37.102
To being an author.

00:06:37.102 --> 00:06:40.439
Making the decision that I could accept

00:06:40.439 --> 00:06:44.409
the rolling of the eyes
of the Ivy League grads

00:06:44.409 --> 00:06:48.728
or the establishment to get bigger reach,

00:06:48.728 --> 00:06:51.259
to build audience to jam with people,

00:06:51.259 --> 00:06:54.421
to be human and extroverted and

00:06:54.421 --> 00:06:57.146
taking that step back

00:06:57.146 --> 00:07:00.702
or two or three in the minds

00:07:00.702 --> 00:07:03.554
of the top two or three percent

00:07:04.634 --> 00:07:08.255
was a pretty interesting,
difficult decision for me.

00:07:08.255 --> 00:07:10.623
Knowing what the consequences were of

00:07:10.623 --> 00:07:13.131
putting myself out there
and that I would be

00:07:13.131 --> 00:07:16.196
respected less for my
business accomplishments

00:07:16.196 --> 00:07:17.496
because of it

00:07:17.496 --> 00:07:19.818
wasn't the easiest thing
that I ever had to deal with

00:07:19.818 --> 00:07:21.420
and something that's very honestly I still

00:07:21.420 --> 00:07:22.349
continue to deal with.

00:07:22.349 --> 00:07:25.669
I, now, this is where you
start getting into the,

00:07:25.669 --> 00:07:27.880
I don't know, DRock, if you 
wanna make it dark.

00:07:27.880 --> 00:07:29.106
But this is where you start
getting into the darkest stuff.

00:07:29.106 --> 00:07:32.670
In a weird way, I like the underestimation

00:07:32.670 --> 00:07:33.773
that comes along with it as well.

00:07:33.773 --> 00:07:36.304
Because the truth is the
reason that entrepreneurship

00:07:36.304 --> 00:07:40.204
and business speaks to me
especially in an American context

00:07:40.204 --> 00:07:42.596
is that the truth is undefeated.

00:07:42.596 --> 00:07:45.590
That ultimately, you
know, if I go and execute

00:07:45.590 --> 00:07:47.913
multiple hundred million dollar businesses

00:07:47.913 --> 00:07:49.330
and make smart investments,

00:07:49.330 --> 00:07:50.862
it's all net net, right?

00:07:50.862 --> 00:07:52.650
Like you may not like
the fact that I don't

00:07:52.650 --> 00:07:56.597
dress up or that I curse on
stage or that I self promote

00:07:56.597 --> 00:07:58.222
or whatever you wanna call it.

00:07:58.222 --> 00:08:00.730
But if I execute, you just have to eat it.

00:08:00.730 --> 00:08:05.211
And so that was clearly,
DJ, that was clearly

00:08:05.211 --> 00:08:06.976
the toughest thing for me, which was that

00:08:06.976 --> 00:08:10.784
I was consciously self aware
that I was entering a realm

00:08:10.784 --> 00:08:14.081
where I would start
becoming more disrespected

00:08:14.081 --> 00:08:16.612
even though my accomplishments
were gonna be greater.

00:08:16.612 --> 00:08:20.610
That mis-positioning was something that,

00:08:20.610 --> 00:08:22.383
that was tough.

00:08:22.383 --> 00:08:23.717
Question of the day.

00:08:23.717 --> 00:08:24.553
Let's just actually, you know what?

00:08:24.553 --> 00:08:25.551
That was such a profound question.

00:08:25.551 --> 00:08:26.271
Question of the day.

00:08:26.271 --> 00:08:27.989
What is the hardest thing you ever had

00:08:27.989 --> 00:08:29.197
to do professionally?

00:08:29.197 --> 00:08:29.870
Because let's be honest,

00:08:29.870 --> 00:08:33.243
there's way tougher
things of raising a child

00:08:33.243 --> 00:08:36.789
with a disability or bankruptcy or pain

00:08:36.789 --> 00:08:38.159
or a lot of emotional stuff.

00:08:38.159 --> 00:08:39.808
So in the context of business,

00:08:39.808 --> 00:08:42.059
what is the toughest business challenge

00:08:42.059 --> 00:08:44.706
that you've ever had to to deal with?

00:08:44.706 --> 00:08:46.123
You keep asking questions.

00:08:46.123 --> 00:08:48.190
I'll keep answering them.

00:08:48.190 --> 00:08:53.190
(lively music)

